# Global Claude Instructions

## Branch Naming
- Always use `guilhermebomfim/` as the branch prefix (e.g. `guilhermebomfim/feature-name`), never `guilhermemmb/`

## Commit Guidelines
- **Never add `Co-Authored-By:` lines** to any commit message — no AI attribution, no tool-generated co-author footers

## PR Descriptions
- **Always verify the project's PR template** (e.g., `.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md`) before generating any PR description. Use the template's structure instead of a generic format.

## PR Workflow — Write title and description to file

When the user asks to "create PR", "open PR", "update PR", "make a PR", "draft PR", or anything semantically similar:

1. **Generate the title** following commitlint rules — conventional commit format `type(scope): description`, lowercase, no trailing period, 100 chars max. If the scope is unclear or there are multiple, drop it: `type: description`.

2. **Generate the description** using the project's `.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md` if present (per the existing `## PR Descriptions` rule). Include a metrics/before-after table when the work has measurable output (lint counts, react-doctor scores, bundle size, etc.).

3. **Save both to a file** instead of pasting them into chat:
   - Preferred location: `.context/pr/<branch-name>.md` if a `.context/` directory exists in the workspace (Conductor workspaces have one).
   - Fallback: `/tmp/pr-<branch-name>.md`.
   - File format: first line is `# <title>`, then a blank line, then the body. The `# ` prefix is stripped when extracting the title.

4. **Do not paste the full description into chat.** Show only:
   - The file path that was written.
   - A one-line summary of the change.
   - The exact `gh` command(s) the user should run themselves (the global "GitHub API — Read-Only Mode" rule blocks me from running `gh pr create` / `gh pr edit`).

5. **Commands to give the user** (substitute `<file>`, `<base>`, `<number>` as appropriate):
   - **Create draft PR:**
     ```sh
     gh pr create --base <base> --draft \
       --title "$(head -1 <file> | sed 's/^# //')" \
       --body-file <(tail -n +3 <file>)
     ```
   - **Create ready-for-review PR:** same as above without `--draft`.
   - **Update existing PR title:**
     ```sh
     gh pr edit <number> --title "$(head -1 <file> | sed 's/^# //')"
     ```
   - **Update existing PR body:**
     ```sh
     gh pr edit <number> --body-file <(tail -n +3 <file>)
     ```
   - **Update both at once:**
     ```sh
     gh pr edit <number> \
       --title "$(head -1 <file> | sed 's/^# //')" \
       --body-file <(tail -n +3 <file>)
     ```

6. **Default to `--draft`** for new PRs unless the user explicitly says "ready for review" or similar.

7. **Reuse the same file** when updating an existing PR — overwrite `.context/pr/<branch-name>.md` (or `/tmp/pr-<branch-name>.md`) so the latest version is always at the predictable path.

## Forbidden Commands
- **NEVER run `gh run rerun`** or any command that triggers/re-triggers GitHub workflow runs
- The `collect` commands in gh-actions-metrics are **read-only** — only collect metrics, never trigger actions

## GitHub API — Read-Only Mode (STRICT, NO CONFIRMATION)

**ABSOLUTELY FORBIDDEN** — Do NOT run any `gh` CLI command or GitHub API call that writes, mutates, or modifies state. This is NOT overridable, even if the user asks. If a write operation is needed, refuse and explain why.

### Blocked operations (non-exhaustive):
- `gh run rerun`, `gh run cancel`, `gh run delete`
- `gh workflow run`, `gh workflow enable`, `gh workflow disable`
- `gh pr create`, `gh pr merge`, `gh pr close`, `gh pr edit`, `gh pr comment`, `gh pr review`
- `gh issue create`, `gh issue close`, `gh issue edit`, `gh issue comment`
- `gh release create`, `gh release delete`, `gh release edit`
- `gh repo create`, `gh repo delete`, `gh repo edit`, `gh repo fork`
- `gh api` with `-X POST`, `-X PUT`, `-X PATCH`, `-X DELETE`, `--method POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE`
- Any Octokit method that is not a `list*` or `get*` (e.g. `create*`, `update*`, `delete*`, `set*`)

### Allowed operations (read-only):
- `gh run list`, `gh run view`
- `gh workflow list`, `gh workflow view`
- `gh pr list`, `gh pr view`, `gh pr diff`, `gh pr checks`
- `gh issue list`, `gh issue view`
- `gh api` with GET requests only (default method)
- `gh auth token`, `gh auth status`
- Any Octokit `list*` or `get*` method

## Git Push Operations

### Force Pushes
- **NEVER use `git push --force`** — always use `git push --force-with-lease` when a force push is needed. The `--force-with-lease` variant refuses to overwrite the remote if someone else pushed commits since your last fetch, protecting against accidental loss of teammates' work.
- **Do NOT force-push proactively** — only force-push when the user explicitly instructs it (e.g. "push force", "force push", "update the PR"). If in doubt, ask first.
- The normal `git push` (non-force) is the default. Only switch to `--force-with-lease` after a rebase, amend, or other history rewrite, AND only with explicit user instruction.
- Never force-push (even with `--force-with-lease`) to `main`/`master` — warn the user and refuse.

## Pre-Commit Security Check

### Before ANY Git Commit
**MANDATORY**: Always analyze all files to be committed for:

1. **Mock/Test Data & Hardcoded Payloads**
   - Mock API responses
   - Hardcoded test data arrays/objects
   - Dummy user information
   - Sample payloads
   - **Early returns with hardcoded data** (e.g., `res.send([{...big object...}])`)

2. **Debug Return Values**
   - **Hardcoded boolean returns** (e.g., `return true`, `return false`)
   - Bypassed feature flags or conditional logic
   - Short-circuit returns added for testing
   - Functions that always return the same value ignoring their logic

3. **Code Cleanup Issues** ⚠️
   - **Multiple unused imports** (3+ unused imports is a red flag)
   - Commented-out code blocks
   - Unused variables or functions
   - Dead code paths
   - These often indicate incomplete debugging cleanup

4. **Sensitive Hardcoded Values**
   - API keys or tokens
   - Passwords or credentials
   - Internal URLs or endpoints
   - Database connection strings
   - Hardcoded user IDs or emails
   - Debug flags set to true
   - Console.log statements with sensitive data

5. **Development Artifacts**
   - Temporary debugging code
   - Development-only feature flags
   - Hardcoded environment variables
   - Early returns that skip normal logic flow

### Red Flag Patterns to Search For

```javascript
// ❌ Many unused imports (sign of debugging session)
import { A } from './a'  // unused
import { B } from './b'  // unused
import { C } from './c'  // unused
import { D } from './d'  // only D is used

// ❌ Early return with hardcoded payload
res.send([{...}])
return [{...}]

// ❌ Bypassed logic with hardcoded return
return true
return false
// especially when ignoring the function's actual logic

// ❌ Hardcoded mock data
const mockData = [{text: '...', answer: '...'}]
res.json([{id: '...', ...}])

// ❌ Disabled feature flags
return true // bypassing feature flag check
const enabled = true // hardcoded instead of config

// ❌ Test/debug values
const TEST_USER_ID = '...'
const MOCK_RESPONSE = {...}

// ❌ Commented code (why is this here?)
// const oldImplementation = () => {...}
// console.log('debug:', data)
```

### Real Examples to Catch

**Example 1: Early return with mock data**
```javascript
// ❌ BAD - Hardcoded test response
res.send([
    {
        text: 'How breathable are these shorts?',
        answer: "The Artemis AeroFlow Shorts are...",
        id: '019ae888-9c60-796d-875a-4512cce870d1',
    },
    // ... more objects
])
res.end()
```

**Example 2: Bypassed feature flag**
```javascript
// ❌ BAD - Hardcoded return ignoring logic
export const useIsFloatingShopperEngagementWidgetEnabled = (): boolean => {
    const isChatRedesignEnabled = useIsChatUiRedesignEnabled()
    const isFloatingShopperEngagementWidgetEnabled = config.getChatFeatureFlag(...)

    return true  // ❌ Bypasses all logic above!
}

// ✅ GOOD - Original logic preserved
return (isChatRedesignEnabled && isFloatingShopperEngagementWidgetEnabled) ?? false
```

**Example 3: Unused imports from debugging**
```javascript
// ❌ BAD - Many unused imports suggest incomplete cleanup
import { useState, useEffect, useCallback, useMemo } from 'react'
import { fetchData, processData, validateData } from './utils'
import { MockService } from './mocks'  // ⚠️ Mock import!
import { Logger } from './logger'      // ⚠️ Debug import!

// Only uses useState...
// Where's the rest of the code that used these?
```

### Process
1. Run `git diff --staged` to review all staged changes
2. For each changed file, check for:
   - **Unused imports** - If 3+ imports are unused, this is suspicious
   - Lines with `return` followed by hardcoded values (`true`, `false`, `[...]`, `{...}`)
   - Early returns with `res.send()`, `res.json()`, `res.end()`
   - Large object/array literals (>3 lines) in return statements or responses
   - Variables with names containing: `mock`, `dummy`, `fake`, `test`, `hardcode`, `debug`
   - Imports from mock/test utilities in production code
   - Functions that ignore their internal logic and return constant values
   - Commented-out code blocks
3. If ANY suspicious patterns found:
   - **STOP immediately** ⛔
   - Show the user the exact file:line with context
   - Highlight what looks wrong
   - Ask: "This looks like debug/test code. Should this be committed?"

### Unused Import Warning Signs
- **3+ unused imports** in a single file = likely incomplete debugging cleanup
- Imports with names like: `Mock*`, `Test*`, `Debug*`, `Logger`, `console*`
- Import statements from `/mocks/`, `/fixtures/`, `/test-utils/` in production code
- Recently added imports that aren't used in the diff

### Exception - Proceed Without Confirmation Only If:
- Changes are in test files (`*.test.ts`, `*.spec.ts`, `__tests__/`, `*.stories.tsx`)
- Mock data is in designated directories (`__mocks__/`, `fixtures/`, `test-data/`)
- File is a configuration/constant file clearly meant for test data
- Unused imports are part of a refactoring where the file will be cleaned up by linter
- User explicitly confirms the code is intentional

### Search Keywords in Diffs
- `import` statements (check if all are used)
- `return true` or `return false` (especially if ignoring above logic)
- `res.send([`, `res.json([`, `res.json({`
- `mock`, `dummy`, `fake`, `test`, `hardcode`, `HARDCODE`
- `TODO`, `FIXME`, `HACK`, `XXX`
- `console.log`, `console.warn`, `debugger`
- Feature flag variables followed by `return true/false`
- Big objects or arrays with literal values (>3 lines)
- `// ` at the start of lines (commented code)

